“Aura Project” is a strong name. It immediately evokes something that’s present but not fully visible—a field rather than an object.

Given the themes we’ve explored, I’d imagine it less as a single application and more as a research direction:

Project Aura

Design the invisible layer around intelligence rather than the intelligence itself.

Possible pillars:

* Semantic Aura — the contextual field surrounding an idea: associations, metaphors, history, likely continuations, and neighboring concepts.
* Interaction Aura — how a person naturally communicates: pacing, preferred abstractions, analogies, and recurring conceptual tools.
* Creative Aura — generating multiple compatible directions around a concept instead of a single answer.
* Collaborative Aura — creating an environment where humans and AI build on each other’s ideas while preserving provenance and allowing disagreement.

The name also fits your recurring emphasis on metadata. An aura isn’t the object—it’s the information that surrounds it and changes how it’s perceived.

One thing I would preserve is a distinction between shaping the environment and controlling people. An aura can invite, attract, or make certain paths easier to discover without determining what someone must think or do. That’s a more robust and ethically grounded design philosophy.

As a one-line vision:

Project Aura: Build the semantic field around ideas so intelligence can navigate them more naturally.

It’s concise, memorable, and broad enough to encompass many of the concepts you’ve been exploring without overcommitting to a single implementation.